--TEST--
`void` and `never` are refused in a parameter and accepted in a return — `rule:types/grammar`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/grammar`: "`void` and `never` are return-only." Neither has an argument
// that satisfies it — `void` is the absence of a value and `never` is the
// empty type — so a method declaring one can never be called at all. It is
// refused at the declaration rather than at every call site.
final class Refused {
    public static function takesVoid(void $p): void {}

    public static function takesNever(never $p): void {}
}

// The rule is the signature's, not the body's: an interface method and an
// abstract one are just as uncallable, and `nvs_types::signatures` is the one
// pass that reads every declared shape.
interface Signature {
    public function alsoRefused(never $p): int;
}

// Both spellings are still return types, and neither is reported here.
final class Allowed {
    public static function nothing(): void {}

    public static function diverges(): never {
        throw new LogicError("unreached");
    }
}
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0742]: `void` is a return type only, and this is a parameter
  --> case.nvs:7:38
%A
error[E0742]: `never` is a return type only, and this is a parameter
  --> case.nvs:9:39
%A
error[E0742]: `never` is a return type only, and this is a parameter
   --> case.nvs:16:33
%A
